  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 PM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nWest/northwest upper flow across the Great Lakes, as shortwave \nenergy ejects eastward out of the western CONUS trough, with \nhigh pressure anchored over south central parts of the country. \nA quasi-stationary boundary over the northern Ohio Valley \ncontinues to be the focusing mechanism for areas of heavy rain,\nwith most of this activity remaining well south of our area. \nCool, Canadian high pressure will become the dominate weather \nplayer as we head into next week, with a modified Rex block over\nthe center of the country as low pressure slips underneath the \nridge. \n\nLow pressure sliding along the boundary to our south will help to \nwobble the boundary a little further north tonight, along with a \nweakish mid level inverted trough swinging across the area.  This \nwill briefly push deeper moisture northward toward northern lower, \nresulting in a few showers (sprinkles?), most numerous south of M-\n72. Still some question marks as to how far north precipitation may \npush given the persistent dry, low level easterly flow.  Either way, \nany rain amounts will be very light. Any lingering precipitation\nends early Sunday, as drier air begins to push into the area \nfrom the north with increasing influence of Canadian high \npressure. \n\nThis begins a relatively benign weather period across northern \nMichigan as high pressure largely keeps any rain chances west/south \nof the area. We will see a few days of seasonably cool \ntemperatures and breezy conditions early in the week, with the \nthermal trough swinging across the northern Lakes. Monday will \nbe the coolest day with highs in the mid 50s to lower 60s, \nsetting us up for a very cool and possibly frosty night in at \nleast some interior areas (if the winds decouple). A gradual \nmoderation in temperatures throughout the week as the surface \nhigh slowly pushes east and weak return flow allows milder air \nto gradually work back into northern Michigan. Minimal rain \nchances throughout the week, with the next better chance of rain\npossibly coming next weekend or early the following the week.",
